# Why does the street walk toward us? Caillebotte's Paris Street; Rainy Day
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | a warm, unhurried gallery guide, quietly amused"}
prompt: "Why are the figures cut off by the frame? Where would we have to be standing?"
next: {"title":"Can this mirror be right? Manet's A Bar at the Folies-Bergère","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/the-painting-knows-youre-there/10-can-this-mirror-be-right-manet-s-a-bar.cast"}

## Paris Street; Rainy Day: where are we standing?
sources:
    - id: aic
      title: Paris Street; Rainy Day (1964.336)
      authors: The Art Institute of Chicago
      year: 2024
      finding: 212.2 × 276.2 cm; a busy intersection a short walk from his home; dramatic cropping possibly inspired by photography; the couple walk toward the viewer, their attention caught outside the frame; shown at the third Impressionist exhibition, 1877
      url: https://api.artic.edu/api/v1/artworks/20684
    - id: wiki
      title: Paris Street; Rainy Day
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2026
      finding: The Place de Dublin crossing near Saint-Lazare; Haussmann's rebuilding of Paris; the lamppost splitting the canvas; Caillebotte born 1848, wealthy patron of the Impressionists
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paris_Street;_Rainy_Day

    card title "Paris Street; Rainy Day"

    image art cites ["aic"] credit "Gustave Caillebotte, 1877. Art Institute of Chicago. Public domain." x 500 y 328 width 860 url https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Gustave_Caillebotte_-_Paris_Street%3B_Rainy_Day_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/1920px-Gustave_Caillebotte_-_Paris_Street%3B_Rainy_Day_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg look screen regions.couple [0.495,0.335,0.415,0.665] regions.couple_feet [0.5,0.9,0.4,0.1] regions.eye_line [0,0.415,1,0.075] regions.faces [0.56,0.4,0.24,0.135] regions.flatiron [0.1,0.05,0.38,0.37] regions.lamppost [0.478,0,0.035,0.75] regions.left_half [0,0,0.49,1] regions.near [0.495,0.17,0.505,0.83] regions.open_left [0,0.38,0.47,0.62] regions.pavement [0.4,0.71,0.52,0.29] regions.right_half [0.49,0,0.51,1] regions.right_man [0.858,0.31,0.142,0.69] regions.umbrella [0.465,0.22,0.39,0.19] regions.umbrellas_meet [0.72,0.17,0.17,0.17] regions.walker [0.335,0.39,0.15,0.34]

Gustave Caillebotte painted this Paris street in 1877, and our que (@highlight art:near@)stion is: why does it seem to walk straight toward us?

It's huge, over two metres tall and almost three wide, and the couple in front is close to life size.
    highlight art:couple

Caillebotte was not yet thirty, a rich young Parisian who showed with the Impressionists and bought their pictures.
    portrait caillebotte x 230 y 470 width 140 of "Gustave Caillebotte" cameo true

    erase caillebotte

The place is real: a new crossroads near the Saint-Lazare station, a short walk from his home.
    highlight art:left_half strength soft lift true

Its tall cream blocks were brand new, part of Baron Haussmann's great rebuilding of Paris.
    highlight art:flatiron strength strong

Down the middle a green lamppost splits the picture in two.
    highlight art:lamppost strength strong lift true

On the left, open space: wet cobbles, and people small and far away.
    highlight art:left_half strength strong lift true

On the right, people so close that the frame can't hold them.
    highlight art:right_half strength strong lift true

    ask "Someone here is cut in half by the frame. Tap him." intro "Now look at the edges." spot right_man on art
        right: Yes: a man seen from behind, only half of him inside the picture.
        wrong: Look along the right-hand edge.

    highlight art:right_man lift true duration 1.2

    camera on ["art@[0.62,0.1,0.38,0.6]"]

Half a man, half an umbrella: the frame cuts wherever it happens to fall, as if nobody arranged it.
    highlight art:right_man

    camera reset true

The couple are cut too. Their feet are somewhere below the bottom edge.
    highlight art:couple_feet effect box lift true

The Art Institute of Chicago, where it hangs, suggests that photography, still new, may have taught him to crop like this. But a crop needs someone looking.
    highlight art:near strength soft lift true

So where would we have to be standing?
    highlight art:pavement strength soft

    quiz "From what height are you looking at this street? Pick one." on_canvas true buttons_at.x 250 buttons_at.y 560
        right: At a standing adult's height: the heads tell you.
        wrong: Compare the heights of the heads, near and far.
        * Up on a balcony
        + A standing adult's
        * Sitting on the kerb

Look at the heads: near or far, almost all of them sit in one narrow band across the picture.
    highlight art:eye_line strength spotlight rest grey lift true

That band is the horizon, and in a picture the horizon always sits at the height of the viewer's own eyes.
    highlight art:eye_line effect box

So we are a grown-up, on our feet, on the same wet pavement as the couple.
    highlight art:pavement lift true

    camera on ["art:couple"] zoom 1.6

And close: they are a step or two away, walking straight at us.
    highlight art:faces art:couple

The man on the right is passing at our shoulder, so near that the frame slices him in half.
    highlight art:right_man

    camera on ["art@[0.45,0.08,0.55,0.45]"]

His umbrella and theirs are about to meet; in a moment someone will have to step aside.
    highlight art:umbrellas_meet

    camera on ["art:faces"] zoom 3

Yet the couple don't see us: they look past us, at something outside the frame.
    highlight art:faces

    camera reset true

So the cut figures aren't careless: the frame is the edge of our own view, and the painting puts us on the pavement, inside the crowd.
    highlight art:near rest paper lift true

    quiz "A film director wants the audience to feel they are inside a busy market crowd. Which shot follows Caillebotte?"
        right: Handheld among the stalls, at head height: the camera stands where a shopper stands, so people brush past it and spill out of the frame.
        wrong: Where would you have to stand for people to brush past you?
        * From a rooftop, the whole market below
        * A long lens from across the street
        + Handheld among the stalls, at head height
        * Every shopper posed neatly in frame

Next time a picture cuts someone off at the edge, ask where the photographer's feet were. That's where it puts yours.
    highlight art:right_man lift true

